><?renderCache.bounds 34736 10953 0 0 0 0 0 0?><?renderCache.textRun 6 NOTICE 0 0 7106 0 0 0 "Arial" 1 0 9000 ISO-8859-1?></draw
><?templateDesigner expand 1?></subform
><draw name="T24" x="95.25mm" anchorType="topCenter" y="150.041mm"
><value
><text
>INSTRUCTIONS ON HOW TO COMPLETE THE COVER SHEET</text
></value
><ui
><textEdit
/></ui
><font typeface="Arial" weight="bold" baselineShift="0pt" size="9pt"
/><traversal
><traverse ref="T22[0]"
/></traversal
><para spaceAbove="0pt" spaceBelow="0pt" textIndent="0pt" marginLeft="0pt" marginRight="0pt"
/><assist role="H3"
/><margin leftInset="0mm" topInset="0mm" rightInset="0mm" bottomInset="0mm"
/></draw
><draw name="T22" y="155.627mm" w="190.5mm"
><value
><exData contentType="text/html"
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/"
><p style="font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:0in"
>To Plaintiffs and Others Filing First Papers.<span style="font-weight:normal"
> If you are filing a first paper (for example, a complaint) in a civil case, you<span style="xfa-spacerun:yes"
>¬†</span
></span
>must<span style="font-weight:normal"
> complete and file, along with your first paper, the Civil Case Cover Sheet contained on pages 1 and 2. This information will be used to compile statistics about the types and numbers of cases filed. You must complete items 1 through 6 on the sheet. In item 1, you must check<span style="xfa-spacerun:yes"
>¬†</span
></span
>one<span style="font-weight:normal"
> box for the case type that best describes the case. If the case fits both a general and a more specific type of case listed in item 1, check the more specific one. If the case has multiple causes of action, check the box that best indicates the<span style="xfa-spacerun:yes"
>¬†</span
></span
>primary<span style="font-weight:normal"
> cause of action. To assist you in completing the sheet, examples of the cases that belong under each case type in item 1 are provided on page 3 of this form. A cover sheet must be filed only with your initial paper. Failure to file a cover sheet with the first paper filed in a civil case may subject a party, its counsel, or both to sanctions under rules 2.30 and 3.220 of the California Rules of Court.<span style="xfa-spacerun:yes"
>¬†</span
></span
></p
></body
></exData
></value
><ui
><textEdit
/></ui
><font size="9pt" typeface="Arial" baselineShift="0pt"
/><para spaceAbove="0pt" spaceBelow="0pt" textIndent="0pt" marginLeft="0pt" marginRight="0pt"
/><traversal
><traverse ref="T22[1]"
/></traversal
></draw
><draw name="T22" y="186.983mm" w="190.5mm"
><value
><exData contentType="text/html"
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/"
><p style="font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:0in"
>To Parties in Rule 3.740 Collections Cases.<span style="font-weight:normal"
> A "collections case" under rule 3.740 of the California Rules of Court is defined as an action for recovery of money owed in a sum stated to be certain that is not more than $35,000, exclusive of interest and attorney's fees, arising from a transaction in which property, services, or money was acquired on credit. A collections case does not include an action seeking the following: (1) tort damages, (2) punitive damages, (3) recovery of real property, (4) recovery of personal property, or (5) a prejudgment writ of attachment.<span style="xfa-spacerun:yes"
>¬† </span
>The identification of a case as a rule 3.740 collections case on this form means that it will be exempt from the general time-for-service requirements and case management rules, unless a defendant files a responsive pleading. A rule 3.740 collections case will be subject to the requirements for service and obtaining a judgment in rule 3.740.</span
></p
></body
></exData
></value
><ui
><textEdit
/></ui
><font size="9pt" typeface="Arial" baselineShift="0pt"
/><para spaceAbove="0pt" spaceBelow="0pt" textIndent="0pt" marginLeft="0pt" marginRight="0pt"
/><traversal
><traverse ref="T22[2]"
/></traversal
></draw
><draw name="T22" y="214.349mm" w="190.5mm"
><value
><exData contentType="text/html"
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/"
><p style="font-weight:bold;text-decoration:none;letter-spacing:0in"
>To Parties in Complex Cases.<span style="font-weight:normal"
> In complex cases only, parties must also use the Civil Case Cover Sheet to designate whether the case is complex. If a plaintiff believes the case is complex under rule 3.400 of the California Rules of Court, this must be indicated by completing the appropriate boxes in items 1 and 2. If a plaintiff designates a case as complex, the cover sheet must be served with the complaint on all parties to the action. A defendant may file and serve no later than the time of its first appearance a joinder in the plaintiff's designation, a counter-designation that the case is not complex, or, if the plaintiff has made no designation, a designation that the case is complex.<span style="xfa-spacerun:yes"
>¬†</span
></span
></p
></body
></exData
></value
><ui
><textEdit
/></ui
><font size="9pt" typeface="Arial" baselineShift="0pt"
/><para spaceAbove="0pt" spaceBelow="0pt" textIndent="0pt" marginLeft="0pt" marginRight="0pt"
/><traversal
><traverse ref="SeePg[0]"
/></traversal
></draw
><traversal
><traverse operation="first" ref="FormNumber[0]"
/></traversal
><draw x="106.21mm" y="237.751mm" w="84.29mm" h="0.164361in" name="SeePg"
><font typeface="Arial" size="9pt" weight="bold" baselineShift="0pt"
/><para hAlign="right" spaceAbove="0pt" spaceBelow="0pt" textIndent="0pt" marginLeft="0pt" marginRight="0pt"
/><value
><text
>SEE PAGE 3 FOR INFORMATION PURPOSES ONLY.</text
></value
><ui
><textEdit
/></ui
><margin leftInset="0.5mm" rightInset="0.5mm" topInset="0.5mm" bottomInset="0.5mm"
/><traversal
><traverse ref="pageNo[0]"
/></traversal
